511c-a972-9afe90baff32" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ffeba9c-70a6-5328-9dc3-5020e0313ad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333595} {:answer/id #uuid "4eafe18b-8c02-5577-a960-94ba6f971ec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333711} {:answer/id #uuid "7213fed3-dd70-59f5-9da6-eb714e406490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334021} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc0b081-37ac-56aa-8ceb-99b894130c9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334379} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb4516a-8d82-5f18-ae27-7aaf1618cf06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ffeba9c-70a6-5328-9dc3-5020e0313ad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333595} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0192dd7-c731-5b69-8d4c-3c9bab7026de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "555c1e17-369e-580d-8f06-901f476b3eee" :question/math-academy-id "q-65064" :question/problem "![](images/45/455df038e550d78fc29463c1a2e22dd44838d24af7428a824f9046fecc1ac1db.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $csc⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/44/44a555d222e3f6ff85208e8722a728ad240c558de7982d2a480bf9ddfb23ac6f.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{15}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{10}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $csc⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{sin⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}csc⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5})}=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}csc⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
